	abstract = {With the widespread adoption of encryption protocols in enterprise networks, traditional traffic inspection methods face significant challenges due to the limited visibility of payload information. Consequently, accurately detecting anomalies in encrypted traffic has become increasingly difficult. To address this issue, this paper proposes an enterprise network encrypted traffic anomaly detection method based on a Contrastive Learning Transformer. Specifically, encrypted traffic is first represented through statistical and temporal features without relying on payload inspection. A Transformer-based architecture is then employed to capture long-range dependencies and sequential patterns within traffic flows. Furthermore, a contrastive learning framework is introduced to enhance feature representation by maximizing the similarity between positive pairs and distinguishing negative samples, thereby improving the model's discriminative capability. During detection, the learned representations are utilized to identify anomalous traffic patterns effectively. Experimental results on benchmark datasets demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms several state-of-the-art approaches in terms of F1-score, Matthews Correlation Coefficient (MCC), balanced accuracy, and False Alarm Rate (FAR), while maintaining strong robustness and generalization performance. The findings indicate that the proposed approach provides an effective and scalable solution for encrypted traffic anomaly detection in enterprise networks. © 2026 The Authors.},
