	abstract = {Driver abnormal behaviors are a major factor affecting driving safety. Although deep learning-based methods have achieved promising results in driver abnormal behavior detection, their performance remains limited under occlusion and small-object conditions in complex driving environments. To address these challenges, this paper proposes RGFE-Net, a driver abnormal behavior detection framework based on a residual grouped self-attention network and a local enhancement pyramid. Inspired by multi-view theory, a synchronized siamese network is employed to simultaneously extract discriminative information from frontal facial and side body views, while a comprehensive decision module aggregates the extracted features to improve robustness against target occlusion. Furthermore, a residual grouped self-attention block is introduced to construct the encoding backbone, enabling effective local–global feature extraction and enhanced representation of both facial expressions and body actions. To further improve small-object detection performance, a local enhancement pyramid is designed, which strengthens local perception through multi-branch convolution and fuses multi-stage features for richer feature representation. In addition, a multi-view driver abnormal behavior dataset containing seven common categories of abnormal driving behaviors is established. Experimental results show that RGFE-Net consistently outperforms nine methods, achieving a precision of 86.69% and an mAP@0.5 of 90.45% on the MVDD dataset, as well as a precision of 96.2% and an mAP@0.5 of 98.7% on the Drowsy-Driving-Det2 dataset. Our code is available at https://github.com/hua1yijiu/Driver-Abnormal-Behavior-Detection . Copyright © 2026. Published by Elsevier Ltd.},
