	abstract = {Road traffic accidents are a global concern, and dangerous driving behavior is a major cause. Current research faces challenges in indicator selection, temporal modeling, and multimodal data fusion. To address these issues, this study proposes a comprehensive data-feature-modeling solution. Using a multi-source dataset from 40 drivers (naturalistic driving and surveys), we establish a three-dimensional indicator system (driver, vehicle, and environment). CNNs extract deep spatiotemporal features, combined with PCA and silhouette coefficient-based hierarchical clustering to classify driving behaviors into cautious and aggressive types. A gradient integration method quantifies feature contributions and identifies 14 key predictive indicators. We then develop a CNN-long short-term memory (LSTM)-attention mechanism (CNN-LSTM-AM) hierarchical model that captures local spatiotemporal features and long-term dependencies. Experimental results show that the proposed model achieves an AUC of 0.924, significantly outperforming baseline models (LSTM, RNN, MLP). This study provides an interpretable framework and an effective technical solution for the precise prediction and intervention of dangerous driving behaviors. © 2026 The Authors.},
